  "response": " Let's break this down:\n\n1. Alice's statements:\n   - Alice said Bob is lying (A1)\n   - Alice said Carol is telling the truth (A2)\n\n2. Bob's statements:\n   - Bob said Alice is telling the truth (B1)\n   - Bob said Carol is lying (B2)\n\n3. Carol's statements:\n   - Carol said Alice is lying (C1)\n   - Carol said Bob is telling the truth (C2)\n\nNow let's analyze each statement based on who is telling the truth and who is lying:\n\n- A1: If A1 is true, then Bob is indeed lying. But Bob's statement B1 says that Alice is telling the truth, which contradicts A1 being true. So, A1 must be false.\n- A2: If A2 is true, then Carol is telling the truth. Since we know A1 is false (and thus Bob isn't lying), A2 remains true.\n\n- B1: If B1 is true, then Alice is telling the truth (as per A2). But if Alice is telling the truth, her statement A1 that Bob is lying would be false, contradicting Bob's statement B1 being true. So, B1 must be false.\n- B2: If B2 is true, then Carol is lying, but as we know from A2, Carol is telling the truth (since Alice was not lying). So, B2 must be false.\n\n- C1: Since A1 is false (and thus Bob isn't lying), Carol's statement C1 that Alice is lying is also false.\n- C2: If A2 is true (Carol tells the truth), then Bob's statement C2 (Bob is telling the truth) becomes true as well.\n\nSo, Alice made one true and one false statement (A2 and A1 respectively), Bob made two false statements (B1 and B2), and Carol made one true and one false statement (C2 and C1 respectively).",
